import json,html
from pathlib import Path
node=json.loads(Path('output/model-comparison/results.json').read_text())
browser=json.loads(Path('output/model-comparison/browser-summary.json').read_text())['results']
esc=html.escape
summary=''.join('<tr>'+''.join(f'<td>{v}</td>' for v in [r['model'],f"{r['top1']}/103",r['correct'],r['wrong'],r['abstained'],f"{r['medianMs']:.1f} ms",f"{r['p95Ms']:.1f} ms"] )+'</tr>' for r in browser)
flows=''.join('<tr>'+''.join(f'<td>{v}</td>' for v in [r['model'],f"{r['topicCorrect']}/103",f"{r['boundaryPass']}/20",f"{r['needsCorrect']}/16",f"{r['pathwayCorrect']}/16",f"{r['suggestionsWithRelevant']}/16",f"{r['irrelevantOffers']}/{r['offerCount']}"])+'</tr>' for r in browser)
capabilities=''.join('<tr>'+''.join(f'<td>{v}</td>' for v in [r['model']['label'],r['model']['tokenBudget'],f"{r['weightsBytes']/1e6:.2f} MB",r['model']['pooling'],r['longInput']['chunks'],'All text preserved','Yes' if r['longInput']['tailFeesInTop3'] else 'No',f"{r['summary']['medianMs']:.2f} ms"])+'</tr>' for r in node['results'])
records=[]
for i,c in enumerate(node['results'][0]['skillResults']):
 cells=[]
 for model in node['results']:
  row=model['skillResults'][i];d=row['decision'];cell='No selection' if d['status']!='selected' else ('Correct' if d['toolId']==c['expected'] else d['toolId'])
  cells.append(f'<td>{esc(cell)}<small>Ranked first: {row["rank"][0]["toolId"]}</small></td>')
 records.append(f'<tr><td>{c["expected"]}</td><td>{esc(c["text"])}</td>'+''.join(cells)+'</tr>')
text='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Yuzee — L6, L12 and BGE comparison</title><style>*{box-sizing:border-box}body{font:16px/1.65 system-ui;color:#273148;margin:0;background:#fff}main{max-width:1220px;padding:44px 24px 80px;margin:auto}h1{font-size:36px;line-height:1.2}h2{font-size:24px;margin-top:42px}p,li{max-width:970px}.eyebrow{color:#7444ad;font-size:13px;letter-spacing:.07em;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:650}.intro{font-size:21px}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;text-align:left}td,th{padding:13px 11px;border-bottom:1px solid #dce1e9;vertical-align:top}th{background:#f7f8fb;font-size:13px}small{display:block;color:#69758a;font-size:12px}.scroll{overflow:auto}a{color:#6940ad}.note{border-left:3px solid #9c80ca;padding-left:18px}input{font:inherit;padding:10px;border:1px solid #bac3d1;border-radius:6px;width:min(100%,480px);margin:8px 0 20px}summary{cursor:pointer;color:#6940ad}code{font-size:.85em}@media print{input{display:none}main{padding:0}tr{break-inside:avoid}}</style><main><div class="eyebrow">Yuzee · measured 17 September 2026</div><h1>Keep L6 as the default. L12 is the more cautious alternative.</h1><p class="intro">L12 and BGE-small are downloaded and available in Settings and Lab. All three were tested against the same 103-skill catalogue and the current chat routing logic.</p><p><b>Recommendation:</b> retain MiniLM-L6 for the current experience. It is faster and routes more of the test requests correctly. L12 makes fewer wrong selections but declines more requests. BGE ranks the expected skill first most often in the browser and accepts longer inputs, but the current confidence gates suppress most of its suggestions. It needs separate calibration before it can be called a better replacement.</p><h2>Actual browser results</h2><p>WASM, quantised q8, one thread, on this computer. “Ranked first” ignores the confidence gate. “Correct routed” is the exact expected skill actually selected after the gate. A no-selection result leaves Gemini to handle the conversation; it does not mean the user receives no answer.</p><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Expected skill ranked first</th><th>Correct routed</th><th>Wrong routed</th><th>No selection</th><th>Median request</th><th>95th percentile</th></tr></thead><tbody>SUMMARY</tbody></table></div><p>Timing covers completed worker requests in this mixed test set. It excludes model download and initial indexing. It is one measured run, not a universal device speed guarantee. Browser and native CPU results differ slightly, so browser results drive the recommendation.</p><h2>How each flow behaved</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Correct topic selections</th><th>Unrelated / boundary requests rejected</th><th>Input-need classifications</th><th>Pathway decisions</th><th>Responses with a relevant suggestion</th><th>Irrelevant / total suggestions</th></tr></thead><tbody>FLOWS</tbody></table></div><p>All models missed pathway prompts phrased as moving from retail into IT without knowing the route, and returning to work after raising children. BGE also missed the beginner-to-software-job route and the school-leaver pathway example. In the browser, BGE incorrectly matched a bedtime-story request to Go Deeper. Several strict skill-ID errors involve adjacent concepts, such as experience options versus comparison, or institution specialisations versus course specialisations; these still need routing refinement.</p><h2>Capabilities and input length</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>App token budget</th><th>Downloaded weights</th><th>Embedding method</th><th>Long-test chunks</th><th>Text retained</th><th>Final fee topic in top 3, native CPU</th><th>Native CPU median</th></tr></thead><tbody>CAPABILITIES</tbody></table></div><p>All three produce 384-number embeddings and can drive the same skill, suggestion, clarification and pathway checks. BGE successfully embedded a 322-token probe in the browser. MiniLM-L6 and L12 correctly rejected it under the app’s 256-token budget. The long-response test split text into measured chunks and retained every character for all models; retaining text does not guarantee that every topic is recognised.</p><p>These models rank semantic similarity. They do not write deeper counselling answers, check factual truth, retrieve live fees or independently reason about eligibility. Those capabilities still depend on Gemini, the approved skill instructions, available conversation context and connected data sources.</p><p>Model-specific handling matters: <a href="https://huggingface.co/BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5">BGE’s published guidance</a> uses CLS embeddings; <a href="https://huggingface.co/sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L12-v2">MiniLM-L12’s model card</a> describes mean pooling and a default 256-word-piece limit. This implementation uses those methods and counts tokens before embedding. It does not copy the upstream one-method-for-all implementation.</p><h2>What was tested</h2><ul><li>103 individually phrased skill queries, covering every user-facing skill.</li><li>The same 103 queries against the richer topic-selection index.</li><li>20 out-of-scope or boundary requests, 16 input-need scenarios, 16 pathway scenarios, 16 response suggestion examples and one medium-input probe per browser model: <b>825 browser cases total.</b></li><li>A separate native CPU run, plus long-input preservation and tail-topic checks.</li><li>All 17 existing regression suites, the new model-switching suite and the production build.</li><li>Main-app Settings switching through L12 and BGE, with L6 restored as default after verification.</li></ul><p class="note"><b>Limits:</b> these are synthetic, task-labelled cases, not 103 live Gemini counselling sessions or real-user accuracy estimates. The same existing similarity and margin thresholds were kept for all models. No thresholds were tuned on this evaluation. A lower selection rate can reflect score calibration rather than poorer embeddings. The next improvement is a separate calibration set with real follow-up context, followed by an untouched evaluation set. No claim of production-wide superiority is justified by this small benchmark alone.</p><h2>Implemented changes</h2><p>The model selector is available in Settings and Lab. Switching stops the old worker, settles pending requests and rebuilds the skill, recommendation, input-need and pathway indexes. Invalid model IDs are rejected, stale workers cannot overwrite the new state, and the 103 skill rules remain active. Public model revisions are pinned for repeatable tests. The current implementation uses browser WASM; GPU performance was not tested.</p><h2>Every skill: native CPU diagnostic results</h2><p>This table is from the separately saved CPU evaluation, not the browser totals above. Exact intended IDs are intentionally strict. Search a skill or query to inspect the differences.</p><label for="search">Find a skill or scenario</label><br><input id="search" placeholder="COURSE_011, apprenticeship, skills…"><div class="scroll"><table id="cases"><thead><tr><th>Expected skill</th><th>Test query</th><th>L6</th><th>L12</th><th>BGE-small</th></tr></thead><tbody>RECORDS</tbody></table></div><h2>Evidence files</h2><p><a href="/output/model-comparison/results.json">Complete native CPU results</a> · <a href="/output/model-comparison/browser-summary.json">Recorded browser totals</a> · <a href="/output/model-comparison/regression.json">Regression results</a></p><p>GitHub changes reviewed through <a href="https://github.com/ashik-yuzee/Yuzee-AI-Lab/commit/890aeff">890aeff</a>. Model switching was integrated into the current worker so the newer local features were retained.</p></main><script>document.querySelector('#search').addEventListener('input',e=>{const q=e.target.value.toLowerCase();document.querySelectorAll('#cases tbody tr').forEach(r=>r.hidden=!r.textContent.toLowerCase().includes(q));});</script></html>'''
Path('output/html/Yuzee-Routing-Model-Comparison.html').write_text(text.replace('SUMMARY',summary).replace('FLOWS',flows).replace('CAPABILITIES',capabilities).replace('RECORDS',''.join(records)))
print('Comparison report built')
